2026-09-01 #29 INSTRUMENT REPAIR — `bin/probe-conversion-candidates.py`, the campaign's candidate filter, has been mis-reporting 38% of the mirror surface. THREE bugs, all found by running it tree-wide and reading the verdicts instead of trusting them. (1) RE-INDENTATION: the port stripped a FIXED four spaces from every live body line and prefixed the mirror stub's indent. For a METHOD that is the identity; for a MODULE-LEVEL function the mirror indent is EMPTY, so the body was flattened to column 0 and the ported file did not PARSE — the harness then reported the SyntaxError as `L3TC-FAIL ['expected an indented block (got …)']`, i.e. as a real conversion blocker. MEASURED: 133 of 352 L3TC-FAIL verdicts tree-wide (38%) were this bug — every module-level stub in pycsl.py (29), pure_ast.py (24), ir_resolve.py (15), monomorphize.py (14), audit_proof.py (12), audit_proof_reverify.py (11), canonical.py (11), sertop.py (6), normalize.py (6). VERIFIED by porting one of them by hand (`ir_inline._inline_calls`): parses fine, and its REAL blocker is a `int -> option int` vs `int` type clash, nothing like a syntax error. (2) The SAME fixed-4 dedent in the RESULT-ERASURE detector's `ast.parse` raised an UNCAUGHT IndentationError that ABORTED the whole sweep partway through pycsl.py and ir_resolve.py — so those files were never fully measured at all. (3) TAIL HEURISTIC: the recorded reason was "the last non-empty line of stdout+stderr", but why3 prints its `File "…", line N` locator BEFORE the message and a long tail of `Warning, … unused variable` lines AFTER it, and a pipeline traceback echoes a SOURCE line last — so 126 candidates recorded `_union_c8_walk(s.get("body", …))` as their blocker. Now it prefers the first line that reads like a diagnosis. LESSON (bd): a MEASUREMENT INSTRUMENT is a claim like any other, and its failure mode is the worst kind — it reports a HARNESS bug in the vocabulary of a REAL boundary, so every reader downstream inherits a fabricated wall. The tell was structural and visible without any domain knowledge: 133 "type errors" that were all the same PYTHON SyntaxError.
2026-09-01 #29 INCREMENT 4 (cheap planes green, proof starting): 477 -> 468, the NINE loop-carrying `_Unparser` visitors, unblocked by a NEW and SOUNDER capability than the one the record named. The recorded reopening for the `for`-over-collection termination boundary was "promote `iter_length` and the node-field projectors to pure `val function`s". THAT WOULD BE A DETERMINISM CLAIM (`len` of an int-collapsed handle is constant only if the object is never mutated) — the exact class of claim this campaign has twice caught as a live unsoundness. INSTEAD: HOIST THE LENGTH. Bind it once, before the loop, to an IMMUTABLE `let _len<idx> = <program length call> in`, use that name in BOTH the guard and the variant. An immutable let-bound int is legal in a logic term, so the variant discharges with NO purity assertion about `iter_length` at all — and it is strictly closer to Python, which takes its iterator ONCE at loop entry instead of re-evaluating the bound every iteration as the previous lowering did. TWO GATES ON THE HOIST, and the first is load-bearing for SOUNDNESS: (a) the length term must contain NO mutable deref — hoisting FREEZES the bound, and for `!hi` (a ref the body may itself assign) that is a real semantic change, not an optimisation; measured, this excludes `iter_length (get_clauses !c)` in Module3_Weaver and `irlen (elts_of !inner)` in Module5_IREmitter; (b) not an @mutable_state class, which already gets the variant unconditionally — excluding it keeps every @mutable_state mirror byte-identical. WITHOUT gate (a) the first attempt hoisted `!hi` in Module3_Weaver, which would have been a silent semantic change; the sibling-emission sweep caught it as an unexpected 4-mirror blast radius. CORPUS: byte-diff is NOT 0 — exactly THREE files change (0418, 0884, 0886) and each diff is EXACTLY the three intended lines (the `let _len… in`, the `invariant { 0 <= !idx }`, the `variant { _len… - !idx }`), nothing else; M1 discipline satisfied. 0418 and 0886 are `# pycsl