03:40 #44: *** UNSOUNDNESS ROUTE #22 DEMONSTRATED — `getattr(obj, "field")` ERASES A DECLARED FIELD TO A LITERAL, and the prose beside it says the opposite. *** Came straight off the ladder's `computed-rhs-erasure = 2` entry: its two sites are `pure_ast._Unparser.unparse_inner` and `expressions._handle_field_get_expr`'s `_pg2 = getattr(self, "_property_getters", None)`. Chasing why a getattr folds to a constant found the lowering, `expressions._lower_getattr`, and its written soundness argument: "the dynamic-config case ... getattr DOES return default for an absent attribute, so this is sound; the actual runtime value is opaque to the prover (fails-safe: any contract depending on the real value fails to prove, NEVER PROVES FALSE)". THE PROBE (scratchpad/w9/probes/g1.py): a class with a declared `self.a: int = 7`, and `def get(self): v = getattr(self, "a", 0); if v: return 7; return 0` under `#@ ensures \result == 0` prints "[+] Verification SUCCESS! All contracts formally proven." while Python returns 7. THE EMISSION IS UNAMBIGUOUS — `type c = { mutable a: int }` declares the field, and the body lowers to `let v = ref 0 in v := 0` — the attribute read is GONE, replaced by the literal default; the `if` is then decided by a constant. THE CONTROL LOCALISES IT EXACTLY: the identical file with `v = self.a` correctly FAILS. THE 2-ARG FORM IS WORSE AND ALSO PROVES: `getattr(self, "a")` has no default at all and the code path `if len(args_ir) <= 2: return "0"` FABRICATES a zero. So the claim "never proves false" is false in both spellings: the default is a WRONG value, not an unknown one — which is precisely what #33's own `_refine_tuple_return_type` comment already said about ONE method ("folded to the LITERAL 0, so the restore put 0 back instead of the saved value — a WRONG value, not an unknown one") without anyone generalising it. CENSUS (env-gated counter on the fall-through, mirror emission of all 53 files + both corpora): 28 fall-through sites in the mirror, 1 in the