10:40 #44: *** ROUTE #24 CLOSED — `computed-rhs-erasure` 2 -> 1, and the lesson is sharper than the fix. *** The generic expression fall-through for a call whose CALLEE is not a plain name returned the LITERAL `0`. PROBE (0996): `o = type(self)()` then `if o: return 7` under `#@ ensures \result == 0` printed SUCCESS while Python returns 7 — an object is ALWAYS truthy and `0` never is, so the model took a branch Python cannot take. CONTROL: `o = C()`, a statically named constructor, always FAILED. *** THE TRANSFERABLE PART IS WHICH READ EXPOSED IT. *** The SAME erasure with a plain field read (`return o.v`) did NOT prove, because the getter is abstract and the result unconstrained — I ran that probe FIRST, saw it fail, and would have written the site off as fails-safe. It was only the TRUTHINESS test that exposed it. So the defect was never that the value is lost; it is that THE MODEL GOT TO DECIDE A BRANCH ON A VALUE IT HAD INVENTED. That gives the campaign a sharper rule than "probe the erasure": AN ERASURE TO A LITERAL IS ONE `if` AWAY FROM A FALSE PROOF, AN ERASURE TO AN OPAQUE VALUE IS NOT — and the way to probe one is to CONSUME it in a guard, not to read it. Three routes this window rest on exactly that distinction (#22's getattr default, #24, and #33's own `_refine_tuple_return_type` note that named it without generalising). FIXED as an APPLIED program `val opaque_dynamic_call (u: int) : int` — Why3 gives each APPLICATION a fresh unconstrained result, so nothing proves in either direction, which is what the erasure's comment always claimed. A `val` CONSTANT would have been wrong for a different reason: two distinct dynamic calls would then be provably EQUAL. NOT made a capability, deliberately: `type(self)` is the RUNTIME class, which under inheritance need not be the statically known one, so lowering it to that class's constructor would trade a demonstrated unsoundness for a subtler one. GATES: census exactly ONE site tree-wide and ZERO in either corpus; bot