02:00 UTC #44: *** THREE MORE ROUTES DEMONSTRATED IN ONE SWEEP — #25, #26, #27 — AND THEY ARE ONE DEFECT, NOT THREE. *** Applied route #24's sharpened rule ("probe an erasure by CONSUMING IT IN A GUARD") to every literal-0 fall-through, batch-style, and three of nine shapes proved a false postcondition where Python returns 7: (#25) a GENERATOR EXPRESSION bound to a local, `g = (i for i in [1,2,3]); if g: return 7` — the site's own comment argues "GenExp stays exactly as inert as it was", and inert is not sound; (#26) a NON-EMPTY SET LITERAL, `s = {1,2,3}; if s:` — emitted as `let s = ref 0 in` with NO STORE AT ALL, the assignment simply vanishing; (#27) a NON-EMPTY TUPLE LITERAL, `x = (1,2); if x:` — emitted `x := 0`. THE SIX THAT ARE SOUND ARE AS INFORMATIVE AS THE THREE THAT ARE NOT: dict literal, list literal, str literal, list comprehension, `zip`, `enumerate`, `range` and `.keys()` all correctly FAIL, and so does every non-truthiness CONTROL — `if 1 in s`, `if x[0] == 1`, `if len(...)` — which localises the defect precisely. It is never that the value is lost; every one of these locals is opaque enough to defeat a read. It is that A LOCAL WHOSE INITIALISER THE MODEL ERASES TO THE LITERAL `0` IS DECIDABLY FALSE IN A GUARD, WHILE THE PYTHON OBJECT IS ALWAYS TRUTHY. PLANNED FIX, ONE CHANGE FOR ALL THREE AND FOR ANY FUTURE MEMBER: track locals whose lowered initialiser is exactly `"0"` while the source RHS is a non-empty collection / generator / unmodelled expression, and REFUSE in `_to_bool` when such a local is consumed as a boolean. Narrow (boolean contexts only), general (closes the class rather than three points), and BYTE-INERT BY CENSUS: a read-only AST scan of both corpora, `src/pycsl`, `src/self-annotate/src`, `src/pycsl_lib` and `tests/` finds ZERO locals of these kinds used as a boolean anywhere in the tree (`scratchpad/w9/census_erased_guard.py`). NOT chosen: making the erased local OPAQUE like route #24 — it is strictly better behaviour but it moves ev