04:15 UTC #45: TWO FRAME QUESTIONS PROBED AND SETTLED — RECORD THEM SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES THEM. (1) `#@ assigns a[0..1]` ON A CONCRETE FUNCTION IS NOT A CHECKED OBLIGATION IN THE VALUE MODEL. A function that declares that region and writes `a[2]` VERIFIES — the region clause is not emitted at all, no `writes` appears in the contract, and WHY3'S OWN INFERRED EFFECTS carry the frame instead. That is why it is not a route, and I probed the only thing that would have made it one: a caller that relies on the stale `b[2]` FAILS, because Why3 infers that `g` writes `b` whatever the clause says. So a wrong `assigns` region on a concrete function is a documentation error, not a soundness hole — and the `clause-survival` deficit it produces (`requires 38->20` on 0661/0662) is of the same benign kind. (2) `#@ \trusted` ON A DRIVER'S OWN FUNCTION IS AN UNPOLICED FRAME. A `\trusted` stub declaring `assigns \nothing` while writing `a[0]` lets a caller keep the stale value and prove `\result == 7` where Python returns 9. THAT IS THE DOCUMENTED MEANING OF `\trusted` — the reviewer asserts the contract — and it is exactly the surface `check-trusted-frame-honesty` measures (0 model-visible of 1 trusted and of 94 converted). THE SCOPE DISTINCTION IS WORTH STATING PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS THE WHOLE ARCHITECTURE IN ONE SENTENCE: for a CONCRETE function the frame is INFERRED by Why3 and is conservative; for a `\trusted`/`\abstract` one it is DECLARED and is trusted. The plane polices the mirror's declarations; a corpus driver's own `\trusted` is the driver author's assertion and the tool makes no claim about it.
