13:02 UTC #51: ROUTE #53 — THE REPAIR IS SPIKED, MEASURED, AND ITS COST IS A REAL ONE I AM NOT HIDING. CENSUS-FIRST PAID OFF IMMEDIATELY (lesson (p)): I did NOT need a new construct, because float arithmetic ALREADY routes through a named bridge, and reading it showed the defect is not "float is a real" in the abstract but ONE `ensures` — `module6_whyml/expressions.py:5378` emitted `val float_{add,sub,mul,div}_op (a b: real) : real  ensures { result = (a +. b) }`, which PINS the bridge to the reals' own addition, and the `_in_spec` path emitted the RealInfix `+.` directly. So the model was not merely imprecise about floats, it was EXACT about them, in a language where the exact answer is wrong. SPIKE (isolated worktree at HEAD, why3 on PATH): make the bridge a DETERMINISTIC OPAQUE — `val function float_{nm}_op (a b: real) : real`, no `ensures` — and route the SPEC path through the SAME symbol rather than through `+.`. `val function` (not bare `val`) is load-bearing and is the campaign's existing idiom (`val function pycsl_none`, `val function path_join_op`): a bare `val` is not deterministic across calls, so two occurrences of `x + x` would not be equal and EVERY float postcondition would die. MEASURED, ALL FOUR WAYS: (1) route #53 CLOSES — `fl3.py` and `fl4.py` both go from `[+] SUCCESS` to `[-] FAILED or INCOMPLETE` (Unknown); (2) CONGRUENCE SURVIVES, which is the whole point of routing the spec through the same symbol — `ensures \result == x + x` for `return x + x` still proves (Valid, 6 steps) and so does the nested `ensures \result == (a + b) * c` (Valid, 6 steps); (3) of the ten float-annotated corpus files, seven are unchanged; (4) *** BUT `0517` REGRESSES AND IT IS A GENUINE COST *** — its `#@ ensures \result >= 0.0` under `#@ requires x >= 0.0` no longer proves, because an opaque tells you NOTHING about ordering. That clause is TRUE of IEEE 754 (a sum of two non-negative binary64 values is non-negative), so this is a real completeness loss, not a false claim being withdrawn. 0517's own docstring says it "proves the additive relationship and a non-negativity bound OVER THE REALS" — over the reals is exactly the unsoundness, so the file documents the defect it locks in. NOT LANDED (suite run 6 is live on `src/pycsl`) and NOT YET DECIDED between two shapes: (a) the plain opaque as spiked, cheapest and obviously sound, cost = every ordering fact about float arithmetic; (b) the opaque PLUS sign-preservation `ensures` that are IEEE-true and decide no equality (`a >=. 0.0 /\ b >=. 0.0 -> result >=. 0.0`), which keeps 0517 whole. (b) is NOT free and I will not take it on assertion: it interacts with route #45 (NaN), because a NaN float has no real representative and a sign axiom is exactly the kind of claim a NaN operand could carry past. That interaction is the next measurement.
13:08 UTC #51: ROUTE #53's REPAIR SHAPE IS NOW DECIDED, AND THE ALTERNATIVE WAS REFUTED RATHER THAN DECLINED. The open question from the previous checkpoint was whether to keep the plain opaque (cheap, loses every ordering fact, costs `0517`'s `\result >= 0.0`) or to add IEEE-true SIGN-PRESERVATION clauses to the bridge so 0517 stays whole. FIRST MEASUREMENT — the NaN interaction, which is why the question was open at all: NaN's ORDERINGS ALREADY FAIL CLOSED at HEAD, measured, not assumed. `x = float("nan"); if x >= 0.0: return 1` and the `< 0.0` twin both fail to prove `\result == 1` (Python answers 0 for both, since every comparison with NaN is False), so a sign clause could not fire on a NaN operand — it would first have to establish `a >= 0.0` for a NaN, which is exactly what does not prove. That looked like a green light. *** IT IS NOT, AND `*` IS WHERE IT BREAKS. *** The sign clauses a reader would write for multiplication are `a >=. 0 /\ b >=. 0 -> r >=. 0` together with `a <=. 0 /\ b <=. 0 -> r >=. 0`. Take `a = 0.0`: BOTH antecedents hold (0.0 is >= 0 and <= 0), so with the mixed-sign clause `a >=. 0 /\ b <=. 0 -> r <=. 0` the model derives `r >=. 0 /\ r <=. 0`, i.e. **`r = 0.0` — a decided EQUALITY, out of clauses that are individually only inequalities.** And Python disagrees: `0.0 * float("inf")` is **`nan`**, confirmed by running it. So the refinement that was supposed to be a free precision win manufactures precisely the class of defect route #53 is about — a wrong answer where the honest answer is none. `+` survives the same analysis (its two clauses can only both fire at `a = b = 0.0`, where `0.0 + 0.0 == 0.0` is true, and `inf + (-inf) = nan` is unreachable because `b >= 0` fails for `-inf`), but a repair that is sound for `+` and unsound for `*` is a per-operator argument, and the campaign's own history — routes #42/#52 — is that a rule which holds for some operands and not others is the shape that gets lost in a later edit. DECISION: land the PLAIN OPAQUE (option (a)), pay `0517`'s non-negativity clause as a measured, documented completeness loss, and record the sign-preservation refinement as the NAMED REOPENING CAPABILITY with its refutation attached, so nobody re-derives it as "obviously free". THE GENERAL LESSON, and it is new: A CONJUNCTION OF INEQUALITY AXIOMS DECIDES AN EQUALITY AT THE POINT WHERE THEIR ANTECEDENTS OVERLAP. `>= 0` and `<= 0` are each harmless; at `a = 0.0` they meet and pin the result exactly. Any future "just add a harmless bound" to an opaque must be checked at the boundary values where its guards coincide, not only in the interior.
