nce-tests.sh` is not (the runner re-invokes ITSELF per test under xargs). WIRING IT INTO THE RUNNER IS NOW BLOCKED ON ONE THING AND ONE THING ONLY — the L1 mirror-sync red — and that is the honest state to leave it in rather than wiring a gate that fails. ALSO IN THIS COMMIT: `check-getattr-erasure`'s `MAX_UNKNOWN` 19 -> 24, and it is JUSTIFIED, not bumped. The five extra sites are route #47's OWN corpus witnesses; the plane's own `--mirror-only` view reports UNKNOWN **19/19 and rc=0** at the same HEAD, which is the instrument rather than my arithmetic confirming that the MIRROR population has not moved at all; DECLARED stays pinned at 0, which is the half that would mean route #22 is back. The reason is written into the file above the constant, naming all five sites, so the next reader does not have to re-derive it.
13:18 UTC #51: THE L1 FINDING GOT SHARPER AND ONE HALF OF IT IS ALREADY REPAIRED IN A WORKTREE. I measured the two divergences by AST STATEMENT COUNT rather than trusting the plane's normalized text diff, and THE PLANE UNDERSTATES ONE OF THEM BY AN ORDER OF MAGNITUDE: it reports `_handle_for_stmt` as "26 missing lines"; the mirror actually carries **71 of the live emitter's 342 statements — 21% of the method** (`_handle_var_expr` is 42 of 48, 87%). A fidelity plane that reports a truncated SAMPLE of the gap instead of its SIZE will be mis-triaged by every reader, mine included — I nearly filed both divergences as the same kind of thing. That is a defect in the plane worth fixing on its own. THE TWO ARE NOT THE SAME KIND. (1) `_handle_var_expr` WAS A STRAIGHT DELETION and IS NOW FIXED: re-inserting the 21 live lines verbatim (the `_iropt_ir_local_vars` read and the `_optional_union_locals` projection arm) brings it to parity and takes the plane from `diverged=2` to `diverged=1`, measured in an isolated worktree at HEAD; the mirror still parses. (2) `_handle_for_stmt` IS NEITHER A DELETION NOR A REFACTOR, AND I REFUTED MY OWN FIRST HYPOTHESIS RATHER THAN SHIPPING IT. The attractive explanation was that the mirror had legitimately DECOMPOSED the 755-line live method, with the split carried by `_classify_iterable` — which in the mirror IS a `\trusted` stub returning `("", "", False)`, exactly the honest technique. REFUTED: `_classify_iterable` exists in the LIVE emitter too, at CLASS level (`src/pycsl/module6_whyml/stmt_control_flow.py:337`), and the live `_handle_for_stmt` ALREADY CALLS IT (`:1169`). So the decomposition is not the mirror's doing and accounts for none of the gap; after delegating exactly what live delegates, 271 statements are still missing. AND IT HAS NEVER BEEN ARGUED FOR: `check-self-annotate-mirror-sync.py` has NO allowlist, exemption or baseline mechanism (grep for ALLOW/EXEMPT/KNOWN/baseline returns nothing), and `_handle_for_stmt` appears NOWHERE in this progress log. It was not an accepted exception; it was never looked at. THE HONEST DISPOSITION, and I am stating it before doing it so the next relaunch can overrule me: `_handle_for_stmt` cannot be called body-verified at 71/342, so either 271 statements get ported and the tree's SLOWEST mirror gets re-proved, or the method is RE-`\trusted` and the metric goes UP by one, 456 -> 457. **The metric rising is the CORRECT outcome here** — the trust surface was always this big; only the bookkeeping said otherwise, and a campaign whose number can only go down has stopped measuring anything. ALSO FOUND WHILE SCOPING THIS: `bin/sync-mirror-bodies.py`, the tool whose entire purpose is this remediation, DOES NOT RUN — it imports `libcst`, which is installed neither in the system python nor in `.venv`. So the campaign's mirror-resync tooling has been unusable for an unknown length of time, which is a plausible part of why a mirror body drifted to 21% without anyone noticing. I did the repair by direct text surgery instead, deliberately NOT by wholesale body replacement, because a wholesale copy would drop the mirror's IN-BODY `#@ loop invariant` lines and turn `check-mirror-loop-annotations` red.
13:22 UTC #51: *** ROUTE #56 FOUND, AND IT CAME OUT OF THE L1 FIDELITY FINDING RATHER THAN OUT OF A ROUTE HUNT *** (838b5e28). While scoping the repair for `_handle_var_expr`'s deleted branches I read what the deleted code DOES, and one of the two branches — the `_optional_union_locals` read projection — carries a comment that names its own defect: "Sentinel picks the carrier's zero (string "", emit_ir `IrOther ""`, real 0.0, else 0)". THE WITNESS, verified by running Python and by the emitted WhyML, not by an agent's say-so: `x: Optional[int] = None` with a branch assignment, then `if x == 0: return 0` else `return 9`, proves `#@ ensures \result == 0` (Valid, 15 steps) while Python returns **9**, because `None == 0` is False. THE MECHANISM, verbatim from `--keep-mlw`: `type _union_f_0 = Arm_0_0 int | Arm_0_None` … `if ((match !x with Arm_0_0 _v -> _v | _ -> 0 end) = 0)`. **THE STORAGE IS FAITHFUL AND THE READ IS NOT** — `Arm_0_None` is a real distinct constructor, and the VALUE-READ projection collapses it to a type-keyed zero, so a `None` local and a genuine `0` become the same term. This is route #44's defect (`None` was the integer 0) coming through a door #44's shared opaque never covers. TIGHTLY BOUNDED BY FOUR MORE WITNESSES rather than asserted: the `float` carrier FAILS CLOSED (`0.0` sentinel, Why3 type error), the `str` carrier FAILS CLOSED (`""` sentinel, "has type string, but is expected to have type int"), the `is None` GUARD is faithful and still proves, and the positive control (`x == 5` after `x = 5`) still proves. So the route is the `int` carrier ALONE — the one place the sentinel and the comparand share a Why3 type. **AND THAT IS EXACTLY WHY ROUTES #50 AND #51 DID NOT FIND IT: this campaign probed the `is None` residue class exhaustively AT `str`, where the failure is a TYPE ACCIDENT rather than a guard.** A class declared safe at one carrier was open at another. Two more facts worth keeping: `docs/pycsl-translational-reference.md:2053-2056` expli