## ROUTE #200 CONFIRMED — a string actual is hashed into an int parameter, and the hash is nameable
`bin/check-argument-coercion.py` — a plane I WROTE this generation — carries this
caveat about the one substitution it left standing:

    "What remains here is the STRING/TUPLE hash (routes #113/#114): it is
     injective-by-luck only, and a caller cannot predict the hash it would have
     to name in a contract to exploit it -- but that is a claim about difficulty,
     not about soundness, so re-probe it if anything ever makes the hash
     predictable."

Nothing had to MAKE it predictable. `stable_hash` is a deterministic function whose
source ships in the repository (`src/pycsl/module6_whyml/identifiers.py`); computing
`stable_hash('"a"') == 747471683` takes one line. The caveat was a claim about an
attacker's difficulty, and the difficulty was zero.

    #@ ensures p == 747471683 ==> \result == 1
    #@ ensures p != 747471683 ==> \result == 2
    def callee(p: int) -> int:
        if p == 747471683: return 1
        return 2

    #@ ensures \result == 1
    def probe() -> int:
        return callee("a")

PROVES. CPython answers 2 (`"a" == 747471683` is False). The TRUE twin
`\result == 2` is REFUSED. Decisive signature, SEV-1.

Note the callee contract shape: `ensures True` on the callee hides this entire
family, which is why 199 routes did not find it. The contract has to be TRUE OF
THE CALLEE'S OWN BODY and READ the property the substitution changes — the method
that produced five of gen #30's routes, applied to the plane's own residue.

