## ROUTE #202 CONFIRMED — the residue route #200 left, and it is user-reachable
#200's repair keys on the DECLARED annotation, because the 46 sites where a string
literal reaches an `int` param across the mirror are `int` BY ERASURE. I wrote that
down as the reason the refusal is free. It is also the hole:

    #@ ensures p == 747471683 ==> \result == 1
    #@ ensures p != 747471683 ==> \result == 2
    def callee(p) -> int:          # NO annotation — erased to `int`
        if p == 747471683:
            return 1
        return 2

    #@ ensures \result == 1
    def probe() -> int:
        return callee("a")

emits `(callee 747471683)` and PROVES. CPython answers 2. TRUE twin REFUSED. SEV-1.

THE REFINEMENT THE MEASUREMENT DICTATES. Keying on the annotation is the wrong axis;
the hash is only dangerous when a CONTRACT CAN READ THE PARAMETER. So the refusal
should fire when a string literal actual reaches a parameter — declared scalar OR
un-annotated — AND the callee's own `requires`/`ensures` MENTIONS that parameter.
That is the exact soundness condition, and it should leave the 46 mirror sites alone,
because their callees are `\trusted` stubs with `ensures True`. MEASURED BEFORE BUILT,
not after.

### Route #202 — why "the contract mentions the parameter" is the EXACT condition, not a proxy
I checked the obvious objection to my own gate before building it. What about a callee
whose contract does NOT mention the parameter but whose BODY reads it, with a contract
that constrains only `\result`?

    #@ ensures \result == 1
    def callee(p) -> int:
        if p == 747471683:
            return 1
        return 2

That contract cannot verify AT ALL: the callee is proved independently of any call site,
with `p` universally quantified, and `forall p. result = 1` is false of its own body. The
call-site substitution cannot rescue it, because the callee's own VC never sees the call
site. So the only way a nameable hash reaches a CALLER's proof is through a callee contract
that MENTIONS the parameter — which is precisely what the gate keys on.

That is the difference between a gate keyed on the reason and a gate keyed on a proxy:
#200 keyed on the annotation because the annotation CORRELATED with danger, and the
correlation is what #202 walked through.

