    def member_of(x: str, *vals: str) -> bool: ...
    member_of("+", "+", "-")

The scanner mapped actual `[1]` onto formal `[1]` = `vals`, found no annotation for it
(a vararg carries none in `param_annotations`) and found `vals` mentioned in the
contract — so the naive gate would have REFUSED a file that verifies today, for a
program with nothing wrong with it. **A vararg formal PACKS every remaining actual into
one sequence; those actuals are not a scalar-parameter mismatch at all.**

The IR says so directly: the function carries `"vararg_str_param": "vals"` (set for an
annotated `*vals: str` AND for a plain `*args`, except when the vararg is
star-forwarded, in which case it is dropped from the model entirely). The predicate now
STOPS at the vararg formal, and the census is re-running.

Worth stating plainly, because it is the third time this generation: the census is not a
formality before the repair, it is the thing that keeps the repair from breaking the
tree. #200's census re-aimed the fix (declared vs erased), #201's census showed the
placeholder was dead in both trees, and this one caught a false positive that would have
turned the suite red.

