## THE KEYWORD SPELLING — MY OWN ROUTE #200 REPAIR HAD THE HOLE ITS OWN LESSON NAMES
Lesson (i), banked THIS generation, says: *a scope justified by "the other case fails
closed" must name WHICH SPELLINGS of the other case were run.* I then wrote route #200's
refusal to walk `node["args"]` and did not run the KEYWORD spelling.

    #@ ensures p == 747471683 ==> \result == 1
    def callee(p: int) -> int: ...
    return callee(p="a")          # keyword, not positional

**PROVES.** CPython answers 2. The IR keeps keyword actuals in a separate slot —
`{"type": "Call", "func": "callee", "args": [], "keywords": [{"arg": "p", "value":
{"type": "String", ...}}]}` — so a check that reads `args` sees an EMPTY argument list
and passes the call.

Found by turning the generation's own method on the generation's own work, one hour
after writing it. It costs one `pycsl.py` run to ask "and what about the other spelling?"
and the answer here was a live SEV-1 in a repair that had already been battery-verified
on the positional spelling.

The #202 patch — not yet applied — now covers BOTH slots, and the keyword case binds by
NAME (which is more precise than the positional index, and needs no vararg offset).

