### A `pkill -CONT` THAT DID NOT RESUME WHAT I THOUGHT IT DID
After resuming gen11 I watched the log sit on seed 8 for eleven minutes and assumed the
seed was slow. It was not: `ps` showed the seed-9 worker in state **T (stopped)**. The
`pkill -STOP -f 'gen11.py'` / `pkill -CONT -f 'gen11.py'` pair matches BY COMMAND LINE,
and the shell running the pkill has the pattern in its OWN command line — so the pair
stops and resumes an unpredictable set that includes the wrapper shells, and a worker
spawned in the window between them can be left stopped with nothing to resume it.
Fixed by `kill -CONT <pid>` on the actual worker; it is back in state S.

>>> A PROCESS-CONTROL COMMAND IS A MEASUREMENT TOO: after `pkill -CONT`, CHECK `ps` STATE
>>> rather than assuming the signal landed. "The log is quiet" has two explanations —
>>> buffering (instrument fact #6) and *not running* — and they look identical from the
>>> log alone. This cost eleven minutes of fuzzing; on a longer job it would have cost the
>>> window.

