### SWEEP LEDGER, second instalment (the CONTRACT machinery itself)
After the value model, the natural next question is whether the CONTRACT machinery that
every route's exploit depends on is itself sound. Four probes, all fail-closed:

  * `#@ requires False` on a callee does NOT leak: the caller must discharge the
    precondition and cannot, so the vacuously-proved postcondition is unusable. This is
    the premise every callee-contract exploit in this campaign rests on.
  * An UNDER-DECLARED `#@ assigns`: a method declaring `assigns self.a` whose body also
    writes `self.b`, with a caller that reads `k.b` afterwards and claims the old value.
    REFUSED — the frame obligation holds on both sides of the call.
  * A `#@ class invariant` that a method's own contract provably breaks: REFUSED. The
    invariant is CHECKED at the boundary, not assumed there (assumed-not-checked would be
    a `requires False` in disguise and would make every method of the class vacuous).
  * GHOST code is isolated in both directions: a ghost value does not become `\result`,
    and a ghost write does not overwrite the real local a postcondition reads.

Plus the trust surface itself: there is NO user-writable `#@ assume`. So the assumption
budget is exactly `\trusted` + `assumes bounded_int` + the Module4-enforced, proof-backed
`fresh_globals` — which is what makes the `\trusted` count a meaningful metric rather than
a proxy for one.

