### I KILLED THE #198 RE-PROOF BY TRYING TO EXTEND ITS DEADLINE. RECORDED, NOT HIDDEN.
`prove198` was wrapped in `timeout 14400`, due to expire at ~06:07Z with the proof still
running after 2h50m. I reasoned that killing the `timeout` WRAPPER would orphan the python
child and let it run without a deadline. **`timeout` forwards the signal to its child**, so
`kill -TERM <wrapper>` killed the proof: `PROVE-RC=143`, 2h50m of prover work gone.

Restarted immediately with `timeout 43200`. The cost is real (~3h of wall clock on a leg
that was already the critical path) and the lesson is cheap:

>>> A `timeout` WRAPPER IS NOT A SEPARATE PROCESS YOU CAN REMOVE — it is the parent that
>>> owns the child's signals. To change a long job's deadline you RESTART it with the new
>>> one; there is no in-place extension. And the time to notice a deadline is too short is
>>> when you WRITE it, not at minute 170 of a run you cannot afford to lose.

The standing fix for the next window: every detached prover run gets `timeout 43200` from
the start. A wrapper timeout is not a proof verdict, so an over-long timeout costs nothing
and an under-long one costs the run.

