not:

    os/__init__.py    getcwd, get_exec_path, chflags, confstr, copy_file_range,
                      getxattr, listxattr, _kill, islink, is_junction      (all `-> 0`)
    stat/__init__.py  filemode  (`-> "----------"`, relabelled this session)
    sysmod/__init__.py  one

Sound today for the same reason as every other stub — the contract is true of the body and
nothing substitutes it for the real module. And this is precisely the set that turns FALSE
the day something does: **a proof that `islink(p) == 0` is a proof that nothing is ever a
symlink.**

AND IT IS OUTSIDE THE NEW GATE'S REACH, which is worth stating rather than leaving as a
silent hole in the coverage: `os` is on `check-stdlib-contract-fidelity.py`'s SAFETY
deny-list, because that gate CALLS the real function and `os.chflags` with a generated
argument is not a test. So this set needs a STATIC gate (a constant body + a contract
pinning it), not a differential one — a different instrument, named and not built, with
the reason it cannot be the one I just built.

## NEW PLANE — `bin/check-stdlib-pinned-facades.py` (the 27th fast plane)
The static companion to the contract-fidelity gate, and a SEPARATE gate for a concrete
reason rather than a stylistic one: the differential gate CALLS the real stdlib function,
so `os` is on its safety deny-list — which puts the sharpest facade set in the whole layer
outside its reach. This one is pure AST, no execution.

THE SHAPE: a body that is a single `return <literal>` **AND** a contract that PINS that
literal. A verified function that computes nothing and claims exactly the nothing it
computes. **870 functions scanned, 12 found**, baselined by name with what removing each
would take. Ten are in `os`; `islink -> 0` is the sharpest — a caller proving
`islink(p) == 0` has proved that nothing is ever a symlink.

One entry is deliberately in the baseline although it is FAITHFUL:
`sysmod.get_float_info_max_10_exp -> 308`, which is the right answer
(`sys.float_info.max_10_exp` IS 308, checked). **The gate keys on SHAPE, not on truth**, so
it flags that function like the others — and the baseline says why it is correct, so the
next reader does not have to re-derive it. A ratchet whose entries explain themselves is
the difference between a list and a record.

Population guard: refuses if the walk sees fewer than 700 functions, so "no facades" can
never mean "I parsed nothing".

