IDENTITY-STUB DEBT: 24 UNADJUDICATED -> 0, AND SEVEN OF THEM WERE FALSE
================================================================================
`bin/check-stdlib-identity-stubs.py` shipped with a debt counter at 24 and a one-line
excuse on each entry ("no citation run", "`os` is on the deny-list"). Measured every one
against this CPython.

WHAT THE EXCUSES WERE WORTH. "csv is on the filesystem deny-list" — `csv.writer` takes any
file-like object, so `io.StringIO` measures it with no filesystem at all, and the answer is
that `writerow(['a','b','c'])` returns 7, the CHARACTER count of `'a,b,c\r\n'`, not the 3
fields the contract pins. The deny-list had never been in the way; it had been an excuse
that LOOKED like a scope statement.

>>> AN EXCLUSION YOU NEVER TESTED IS A GUESS. Check whether the reason still applies before
>>> you write it down a second time.

SEVEN WERE FALSE OF THE CITED FUNCTION (all measured, all now DIVERGES-BY-HAND):
csvmod.write_row, cvar.context_var_get (`cv.get(0)` is 5 after `cv.set(5)`),
cvar.context_var_set (returns a `Token`), dec.getcontext_prec (`prec = 10**30` RAISES
`OverflowError` past `decimal.MAX_PREC`, and the stub guards only `prec > 0`),
nums.rational_num / rational_den (`Fraction(2,4).numerator` is 1, not 2 — `num >= 0` and
`den > 0` do not imply coprimality), os.getenv (`os.getenv('PATH', 0)` is not 0).

TWO OF THE SEVEN CONTRADICT THEIR OWN DOCSTRING. `csvmod.write_row`'s docstring says
"Written bytes >= field count" while its `#@ ensures` pins `== num_fields`;
`cvar.context_var_get`'s says "returns default if not set" while its clause is
unconditional. `os.getenv`'s justification is a COMMENT ("this model has an empty env").

>>> A DOCSTRING THAT DISAGREES WITH THE CONTRACT BESIDE IT IS A FREE ORACLE — SOMEONE
>>> ALREADY KNEW. And a comment is not a contract: the reader who is proving things sees
>>> the clause, not the apology above it.

FIFTEEN WERE A REAL, DECLARED SIZE/COUNT/LENGTH LAW, measured true — which needed a new
class, because calling them DIVERGES would have been as false as calling them FAITHFUL.
DECLARED-DOMAIN requires the declaration to be in the SIGNATURE and the DOCSTRING and the
quantity's claim to be measured; `pkl.dump` is the control that stays DIVERGES-BY-HAND
because its declaration is absent. MODEL-INTERNAL covers the two `os._{en,de}code_name`
helpers, which have no CPython counterpart at all, and names the guard
(`\str_length(name) <= 30`) that keeps the 30-byte field truncation out of the domain.

>>> WHEN A POPULATION WILL NOT SPLIT INTO YOUR CLASSES, THE CLASSES ARE WRONG — but a new
>>> class only earns its place if it comes with a CONTROL that stays out of it.

I RECORDED A RESIDUAL THAT WAS ITSELF FALSE, AND THAT IS THE BIGGER LESSON OF THE PASS.
I wrote — into the plane, into this log and into wall-lesson (h3) — that `hq.heapify_max`,
`heappushpop_max` and `heapreplace_max` "take BOTH `heap: list` and `n: int` and never tie
them", so their size law was TRUE BUT VACUOUS (3-element heap, `n = 99`, proves 99). All
three carry `#@ requires \length(heap) == n`, at hq/__init__.py lines 80, 105 and 114.
They are the BEST-SPECIFIED members of the class I had just invented, and I filed them as
the weakest.

The instrument is the whole explanation: `grep -n -A 4 "def heapify_max("` shows the lines
AFTER the `def`, and a PyCSL annotation block lives ABOVE it. I asserted an ABSENCE from an
instrument that could not have shown the PRESENCE — and the absence flattered the story I
was telling, which is precisely when it goes unchecked.

>>> AN ABSENCE CLAIMED FROM AN INSTRUMENT THAT COULD NOT HAVE SHOWN THE PRESENCE IS NOT A
>>> MEASUREMENT. `-A` after the def cannot see the contract; `-B` is the flag that can.

The correction is kept BESIDE the entries rather than silently replacing them.

MAX_UNADJUDICATED 24 -> 0, which turns the debt counter into a ratchet: a new stub can no
longer be parked there. The "false of the cited function" share is now 31 of 81, up from
the 24 the plane's header previously claimed.

