THE ADVICE AUDIT, SECOND FINDING: AN EXPLICITLY-CALLED DUNDER LOSES ITS CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The `with ... as` refusal (`module6_whyml/functions.py:7166`) advised: "Call `__enter__`
explicitly and assign its result, or use a bare `with <lock>:`". Following the first half
does not work.

THE DIFFERENTIAL, two files identical except for ONE IDENTIFIER:

    class CM:                                  class CM:
        #@ ensures \result == 7                    #@ ensures \result == 7
        def __enter__(self) -> int:                def enter(self) -> int:
            return 7                                   return 7
    #@ ensures \result == 7                    #@ ensures \result == 7
    def read() -> int:                         def read() -> int:
        c = CM(); v = c.__enter__()                c = CM(); v = c.enter()
        return v                                   return v

    FAILED or INCOMPLETE                       Verification SUCCESS

`__len__` behaves the same way; `_enter_` (single underscores) verifies. So it is DUNDER
NAMES, not `__enter__`, and not the `with` machinery.

This is a COMPLETENESS gap, not an unsoundness: the caller cannot use the postcondition, so
the file FAILS rather than proving something false. But a reader experiences "the advice
does not work" identically either way, and the second half of the advice ("a bare
`with <lock>:`") DOES work — measured, under `--memory-model concurrent`.

>>> A REFUSAL THAT OFFERS TWO REPAIRS SHOULD HAVE HAD BOTH TRIED. The one that works was
>>> second; the one that does not was first, and first is what a reader takes.

The message now gives the working repair first and records the measured reason the other
was withdrawn. Witnesses 1784 and 1785 had their census rows refreshed against the new
