A COMPILER FIX OUT OF THE ADVICE AUDIT: `Optional[List[T]]` DID NOT COMPILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The mutable-default refusal told readers to write `xs: Optional[List[int]] = None`. That
shape emits WhyML with an UNBOUND TYPE SYMBOL `array`.

ISOLATED WITH CONTROLS, not guessed:
    List[int] alone              VERIFIES
    Optional[int] alone          VERIFIES
    Optional[List[int]] param    FAILS   (unbound type symbol 'array')
    Optional[List[int]] local    FAILS   (same)
    Optional[List[int]] RETURN   VERIFIES  (no arm goal is emitted there)

CAUSE: `_union_arm_whyml_type` maps a `list` arm to `array int`, the C2/C3 arm goals
quantify over it, and the preamble's `needs_array` is computed BEFORE any body is emitted.
A module whose only `array` is in one of those goals gets no `use array.Array`.

FIXED with the same late-pull route #44 used for abstract ops: a flag set in
`_emit_union_arm_vc` (a `\trusted` mirror twin — no mirror edit, no re-proof) and the
single live-only insertion point that still holds `out`. Byte-inert by construction AND by
measurement: ZERO corpus files use `Optional`/`Union` over any container spelling.

>>> AND THE FILE STILL FAILS — NOW FOR THE REAL REASON, which is what a fail-closed fix is
>>> for. The union TYPE declares `Arm_0_0 int` while the goal quantifies `array int`: the
>>> arm payload type is computed in TWO PLACES THAT DISAGREE. The missing import was hiding
>>> a wrong type behind a wrong error. Recorded as the remaining defect rather than papered
>>> over, and it is a deeper change than an import.

>>> FIXING THE ERROR YOU CAN SEE OFTEN UNCOVERS THE ONE YOU COULD NOT. An unbound symbol
>>> stops type-checking before any goal is attempted, so the FALSE injection goal underneath
>>> had never been reached in the three generations this shape has existed.

