TWO INSTRUMENT IMPROVEMENTS, ONE OF THEM A NEGATIVE RESULT WORTH KEEPING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(1) THE ADVICE PLANE'S STALENESS KEY WAS LOOKING AT THE WRONG END. Its key is the first 140
characters of the unparsed raise — chosen carefully (64 chars COLLIDED 8 times over 198
sites; a line number is noise; 140 is where collisions reach zero). But a refusal's ADVICE
is almost always at the END of the message, so an edit to the advice ITSELF does not move
the key. Found by doing it: clarifying the mutable-default message left its entry looking
fresh and the gate said nothing. Every entry now carries a HASH of the whole message
alongside the readable key, and a key-match with a hash-mismatch is a REFUSAL. Proven to
bite by flipping one stored hash. Banked as (q3).

>>> STABILITY AND COVERAGE ARE DIFFERENT REQUIREMENTS FOR A KEY, and it is easy to solve
>>> the first and believe you have solved both.

(2) THE OBVIOUS WIDENING OF THE DOCSTRING GATE DOES NOT WORK, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS THE
RESULT. That gate keys on a WEAKENING WORD and so missed `csvmod.writerows`, whose
docstring says "Total bytes written" while the clause pins a product — a different
QUANTITY, no weakener. The obvious fix (flag a pinned stub whose docstring names a quantity
noun) was MEASURED BEFORE BEING WRITTEN: 72 hits over the same population, almost all
innocent, because the function's own NAME is usually the quantity (`registry_size`,
`section_count`, `coll.length`). A gate with 72 hits and two defects is a gate nobody reads.

>>> MEASURING A PROPOSED GATE BEFORE WRITING IT IS THE CHEAPEST THING IN THIS WHOLE
>>> DISCIPLINE, and a negative result is worth writing down — it stops the next reader
>>> repeating the experiment, which is most of what a ledger is for.

